{
 "problem": "A sealed, partly saturated soil cell is compressed slowly. Without drainage, the pore pressure follows the strain through the Bishop rate equation; the stabilised fixed-stress coupling used with the PT solver must recover it as the step shrinks, also where the Bishop pressure Se p decreases with suction.",
 "result": "Largest pressure error at 320 steps: 1.43% (moderate suction) and 0.64% (strong suction) in 2D; the error halves with each halving of the step. Without the Bishop correction of the fixed-stress source the error stayed at 78.89% and 67.62%.",
 "scope": "Uniaxial compression to a vertical strain of $--1\\times10^{-3}$ over 1000 s, K = G = 10 kPa, Gardner law; 2D and 3D, 10 to 320 PT steps; 3D at 160 steps: 3.82% and 1.71%. The explicit strain form is exact to 3e-09. A restart halfway reproduces the uninterrupted run exactly; a restart with a quarter of the step stays on the exact path. (a, b) pressure along the strain path; (c) step convergence.",
 "meaning": "This checks the transient Bishop coupling of the PT scheme: the fixed-stress source corrected by $L(\\chi E-D)$, with the increments the mechanics consumed. It does not test flow, which the column checks cover, or large strain.",
